<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ludwig Railway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1835">1835</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Bavarian Ludwig Railway</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ludwig Railway was the first railway in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> worked by steam locomotives, opened on 7 December 1835 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/furth">Fürth</a>, a distance of about 6 kilometres. Named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-i-of-bavaria">Ludwig I of Bavaria</a>, it was built by a private company, and its first locomotive, the Adler, came from Robert Stephenson's works in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. It ran until 1922.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ludwig-railway.jpg" alt="Ludwig Railway" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HerrMay · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karte_Ludwigseisenbahn.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
